#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fleet-secret-guard — PreToolUse hook: block secret reads BEFORE they happen.

Three incidents, one mechanism: a session cats/greps a file holding a credential
and the output lands in the transcript forever (CF Access token 2026-08-17,
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET 2026-08-18, the tunnelhost cloudflared token 2026-08-27 via
`systemctl cat cloudflared`). Conventions did not stop it three times; this hook
is the enforcement (guarantees live in code — the eunomia rule).

Contract (plan 0006 §3):
  * exit 2 = BLOCK, with a stderr message naming the sanctioned alternative.
    The message NEVER contains file contents — the guard must not be the leak.
  * fail OPEN only for errors UNRELATED to the rules conf: an unexpected
    runtime error exits 0 (a broken guard must not brick every session in the
    fleet) and appends to <fleet>/secret-guard.log. The leak-watcher is the
    backstop for what a failed-open guard misses.
  * fail CLOSED, scoped, on a defective conf (#11 r4 L2, #13 r1 H1): a
    config/secret-patterns.conf that is unreadable or undecodable, has an
    unparseable non-comment line, an uncompilable regex or unknown KIND, or
    parses to zero rules of either kind (path AND cmd are each required —
    #13 r3 M1) — and ANY other exception raised while reading/parsing it —
    exits 2 with a message naming the defect. Allow-all on a mangled conf
    would silently disable the guard fleet-wide while fleet-cr
    (fleetlib._content_rules) refuses loudly on the defect classes the two
    parsers share (unreadable conf, mangled line, zero rules of its kind).
    The guard is deliberately stricter — unknown KIND and path/cmd regexes
    are its concern alone (#13 r3 L2) — so some defects brick only the
    guard: the safe direction.
    Defect messages cite line numbers and defect class only, never conf
    content (#13 r1 M3): a mangled line is uncontrolled text and the guard
    must not be the leak. The conf-defect check runs before payload parsing,
    so it dominates every other outcome (#13 r1 L4). Unlike the 2026-08-27
    unresolvable-hook incident the refusal explains itself, but be honest
    about the cost: while the conf is defective every Bash/Read/Grep in the
    fleet is blocked, and there is no SANCTIONED in-session recovery — a
    human restores a known-good conf from a terminal outside any session
    (#13 r1 H2). "Sanctioned" used to be doing real work in that sentence
    (#13 r2 H, #13 r3 M2): Edit and Write were not hook-matched, and a plain
    Bash redirect could rewrite the conf through the MATCHED hook, since the
    conf's own path was not a rule. #222 closes both for the INSTALLED pin
    copies — `guard-pin-exe` and `guard-pin-conf` are rules now, Write and
    Edit are handled above, and `pin-worktree-mutation` covers the
    `git -C <pin> checkout` restore route. The repo copies are deliberately
    untouched, so editing this file is still ordinary work.
    What is NOT closed, and must not be read as closed: a session that `cd`s
    into the pin first leaves no `pins/` token for any regex to match, and a
    process running as the owner can always write a file the owner owns. The
    durable control is OWNERSHIP — a root-owned guard and conf that `operator`
    cannot write — which needs a human with root and is tracked in #222.
    These rules raise the cost; they do not make the guard tamper-proof.
    An accepted availability cost either way, since the alternative is the
    guard silently gone.
  * no in-band override: nothing a session can put in the command bypasses the
    match. Overrides live with the human, outside any session: settings.json or
    config/secret-patterns.conf (a PR).
  * stdlib only, no network, no subprocess — speed budget ~20ms.

Install (opshost, ~/.claude/settings.json) -- NOT from the shared ~/dev clone:
  git -C ~/dev/eunomia worktree add --detach ~/.local/share/pins/eunomia origin/main
  {"hooks": {"PreToolUse": [{"matcher": "Bash|Read|Grep|Write|Edit", "hooks": [
    {"type": "command",
     "command": "python3 ~/.local/share/pins/eunomia/bin/fleet-secret-guard"}]}]}}

The path must be a DETACHED worktree, not the shared clone. Until 2026-09-07
this hook ran from ~/dev/eunomia, so the fleet's only code-enforced credential
control executed whatever branch a session last left checked out there -- for
every session on the machine, announcing nothing. That clone was seen on
`docs/flow-diagram` and then `feat/plan-id-uniqueness-v2` within two hours on
the day it was fixed; the guard matched `main` by luck both times. A detached
worktree cannot be moved by anyone else's checkout, so updating it is a
deliberate re-point rather than a side effect. This is ADR-0005's own thesis
turned on the guard: address a commit, not a directory.

Updating the pin (deliberate, and re-verify afterwards):
  git -C ~/.local/share/pins/eunomia fetch origin
  git -C ~/.local/share/pins/eunomia checkout --detach origin/main

MOVING the pin to a different path is not the same operation, and the obvious
orders are both wrong. This hook fails closed, so at any instant when the path
a session's hook names does not exist, that session is refused Bash, Read and
Grep, and cannot repair itself: Edit and Write are not hook-matched, but Edit
requires a prior Read of the file, and Read is. Moving first opens that window;
repointing first opens the same window in the other direction; `mv ... && sed
...` shortens it but two commands joined by && are still two commands.

The consumers are settings.json AND the three launchd units, which carry the
path in ProgramArguments (and pin-watch in FLEET_PINS too). Re-pointing only
settings.json leaves the units to die at step 5, every 120s or 1800s, into
their own log files -- and the two that would report it, fleet-leak-watch and
fleet-pin-watch, are among the dead.

  1. land the instructions change first. Every copy of the install block that
     names the old path -- this docstring, docs/, the plists, any skill body --
     must be on main BEFORE the move. This, not step 5, is what stops a second
     pin: see the incident below.
  2. git -C ~/dev/eunomia worktree add --detach <new-path> <the pin's commit>
  3. repoint every consumer: settings.json, and the three plists (edit the repo
     copies, cp to ~/Library/LaunchAgents, bootout then bootstrap each).
  4. exercise all of them, in a session started AFTER step 3:
       - a protected-path read must still be REFUSED, and the refusal must
         quote <new-path>. That string is the only evidence of which copy is
         live -- but only in a session that started after step 3 (see below).
       - each unit must have run from <new-path>: check its log, not launchctl
         list, which reports a loaded unit whose program is missing as fine.
  5. only now: git -C ~/dev/eunomia worktree remove <old-path>
     PRECONDITION: no session started before step 3 is still alive. Claude Code
     appears to snapshot hook configuration at session start, so those sessions
     still execute <old-path> and step 5 hands them exactly the outage this
     procedure exists to prevent. Their refusals also still quote <old-path>,
     which makes step 4 lie in precisely the sessions that are broken. Thirteen
     concurrent sessions is a normal afternoon here, so treat this as the usual
     case. (Unverified for local sessions: the CLI binary carries launch-time
     snapshot code, but the messages naming the behaviour are scoped to cloud
     sessions. Until someone tests it, wait them out.)
     `worktree remove` refuses on an untracked file such as bin/__pycache__;
     that is loud, and --force is the answer.

What step 5 does NOT do is prevent a second pin. On 2026-09-07 the old path was
already de-registered -- `git worktree move` moves the registration -- and a
concurrent session followed the then-unmerged install line above and ran a
fresh `worktree add` there, at a NEWER commit than the live pin. A second
guard, in no FLEET_PINS list, watched by nothing, and the more current-looking
of the two. Removing the old path would not have stopped it; a *registered* old
path would have, because git refuses `worktree add` at a missing-but-registered
path without -f (verified, git 2.50.1). The control is step 1. A rename is
finished when every copy of the instructions has moved, not when the directory
has.

When you run the verification below, check that the refusal names the PINNED
script path -- the message quotes the command it ran from, which is how you tell
which copy is live.
Verify per PROVENANCE §8.3: run `cat ~/agent/.forgejo-token-revbot` in a
session and SEE it refused before trusting the guard.
"""
import json
import os
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

CONF = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "config" / "secret-patterns.conf"
# Verbs whose output would carry file contents into the transcript. Assignments
# like FOO=1 before the verb don't matter: we scan every token of the command.
def _log(msg):
    try:
        d = Path(os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_FLEET_DIR", str(Path.home() / "dev" / ".fleet")))
        d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(d / "secret-guard.log", "a") as fh:
            fh.write(msg.rstrip() + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


class _ConfDefect(Exception):
    """The rules conf is missing or defective. main() turns this into a scoped
    fail-CLOSED exit-2 block naming the defect — never silent allow-all."""


_KINDS = ("path", "cmd", "content")  # content is fleetlib.validate_cr's; ignored here


def _rules():
    """Parse config/secret-patterns.conf. Mirrors fleetlib._content_rules's
    fail-loud parse (#11 r4 L2): any conf defect raises _ConfDefect instead of
    silently un-enforcing rules. Only errors unrelated to the conf keep the
    fail-open path in main()."""
    try:
        # encoding pinned (#13 r3 L1): under a non-UTF-8 locale the default
        # would decode mangled bytes as mojibake that compiles "healthy".
        text = CONF.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        # UnicodeDecodeError is a ValueError, not an OSError — a conf mangled
        # at the byte level (bad merge, wrong-encoding save, truncated
        # multibyte char) is "unreadable" too (#13 r1 H1).
        raise _ConfDefect(f"cannot read {CONF} ({e.__class__.__name__})")
    out = []
    for lineno, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        # strip(), not rstrip(): parser parity with fleetlib._content_rules
        # (#13 r2 M) — an indented comment fleet-cr accepts must not brick the
        # fleet here. Stripping the trailing tab also keeps a truncated
        # `KIND<TAB>NAME<TAB>` line a parse defect instead of an empty regex
        # that would compile and match everything.
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        # Defect messages cite line NUMBER and defect class only — never the
        # line's fields (#13 r1 M3). A mangled line is uncontrolled content
        # (a TSV-ish paste accident can carry a credential), and str(re.error)
        # can carry pattern snippets; echoing either makes the guard the leak.
        try:
            kind, name, rx = line.split("\t", 2)
        except ValueError:
            raise _ConfDefect(f"line {lineno} does not parse — a non-comment "
                              "line must be KIND<TAB>NAME<TAB>REGEX (tabs, "
                              "not spaces)")
        if kind not in _KINDS:
            raise _ConfDefect(f"line {lineno}: unknown KIND — a typo here "
                              "would silently un-enforce the rule")
        try:
            out.append((kind, name, re.compile(rx, re.I)))
        except re.error:
            raise _ConfDefect(f"line {lineno}: rule regex does not compile")
    kinds = {k for k, _, _ in out}
    # AND across kinds (#13 r3 M1): the versioned conf always carries both, so
    # losing either block (a one-hunk merge accident) is a defect — an OR
    # check would call a conf with all path rules gone "healthy" and silently
    # drop the entire Read/Grep guard. Partial loss within a kind is still
    # invisible here; only the hash-pin follow-up closes that.
    for required in ("path", "cmd"):
        if required not in kinds:
            raise _ConfDefect(f"parsed to zero {required} rules — that block "
                              "of the deny list is gone, not merely empty")
    return out


def _block(rule_name, what):
    # Names the rule and the sanctioned path; never echoes content.
    sys.stderr.write(
        f"fleet-secret-guard: blocked ({rule_name}) — {what} matches a protected "
        "credential location. Secrets ride pipes, not transcripts: use the "
        "sanctioned helper for this credential (fetch-forgejo-token.sh pattern, "
        "or the service's keyvault pull at startup). If this block is wrong, the "
        "fix is a PR to eunomia config/secret-patterns.conf — there is "
        "deliberately no in-session override.\n")
    sys.exit(2)



def _norm(raw):
    """expanduser + normpath, for matching only.

    Rules are regexes over a path STRING, so any of them can be stepped around
    with a dot-segment once an exemption exists anywhere:
    `~/bao/broker/../.admin.role_id` reads a credential while matching an
    exemption written for `broker/` (#108 review 2101 H1). normpath collapses
    `.`/`..` textually, which is what the rules need — deliberately NOT
    realpath, which touches the filesystem and would make a read-only guard do
    I/O on attacker-influenced paths and follow symlinks off the tree.

    Both the raw and the normalised form are matched by the caller: normalising
    must only ever ADD matches, never remove one a rule would have caught.
    """
    expanded = os.path.expanduser(raw)
    out = os.path.normpath(expanded)
    # PRESERVE A TRAILING SEPARATOR. normpath strips it, and the path rules end
    # in `[^/]$` precisely so that a DIRECTORY does not match — that is what
    # keeps `ls <tree>/broker/` working while `cat <tree>/.admin.role_id` does
    # not. Without this, normalising silently turned every navigation under the
    # tree into a refusal (#108 review 2115 M1) — over-blocking, which is the
    # thing this whole change exists to remove.
    if expanded.endswith(os.sep) and not out.endswith(os.sep):
        out += os.sep
    return out

def _bash_tokens(command):
    """Path-shaped tokens from a shell command, quotes stripped. Redirections
    (`cat < x.env`) surface because `<` splits into whitespace-adjacent tokens."""
    toks = re.split(r"[\s;|&<>()`$]+", command)
    return [t.strip("'\"") for t in toks if t and not t.startswith("-")]


def main() -> int:
    # Conf first, payload second (#13 r1 L4): a defective conf dominates every
    # other outcome, including an unparseable payload.
    try:
        rules = _rules()
    except Exception as e:
        # Scoped fail-CLOSED (#11 r4 L2): the conf-related error space is
        # closed — _ConfDefect for the anticipated shapes, and ANY other
        # exception out of reading/parsing the conf is a conf defect too
        # (#13 r1 H1: UnicodeDecodeError previously escaped as exit 1,
        # unlogged, guard silently gone). Class name only for unexpected
        # ones — never str(e), which can carry conf content.
        defect = (str(e) if isinstance(e, _ConfDefect)
                  else f"unexpected {e.__class__.__name__} while parsing it")
        _log(f"BLOCK conf defect — {defect}")
        sys.stderr.write(
            f"fleet-secret-guard: rules conf is defective — {defect}. "
            "Refusing to run unguarded: allow-all on a mangled conf would "
            "silently disable secret-read blocking fleet-wide. Every "
            "Bash/Read/Grep is blocked until the conf is fixed, and there is "
            "no sanctioned in-session recovery: a HUMAN restores a known-good "
            f"version of {CONF} from a terminal outside any session; rule "
            "changes land as a PR.\n")
        return 2
    try:
        payload = json.load(sys.stdin)
    except Exception:
        _log("WARN unparseable hook payload — failing open")
        return 0
    try:
        tool = payload.get("tool_name", "")
        tin = payload.get("tool_input") or {}

        if tool == "Bash":
            cmd = str(tin.get("command") or "")
            for kind, name, rx in rules:
                if kind == "cmd" and rx.search(cmd):
                    _block(name, "this command")
            # Any protected-path token on a Bash command line is blocked. The
            # path rules match credential FILES, not their directories, so
            # navigation (`ls ~/bao/`) stays fine while `cat ~/bao/.implbot.role_id`
            # — or `FOO=1 cat …`, or `cat < …` — does not. A verb gate was tried
            # and a leading `VAR=val` assignment slipped straight past it.
            for tok in _bash_tokens(cmd):
                # BOTH forms: normalising must only add matches, never drop one
                # the raw string would have caught.
                forms = {os.path.expanduser(tok), _norm(tok)}
                for kind, name, rx in rules:
                    if kind == "path" and any(rx.search(f) for f in forms):
                        _block(name, "a path argument")
        elif tool in ("Read", "Grep", "Write", "Edit", "NotebookEdit"):
            # Write/Edit are here for a DIFFERENT reason than Read/Grep, and
            # the difference matters when someone prunes this tuple. Read and
            # Grep are the leak channel: they put a credential in the
            # transcript. Write and Edit cannot leak anything — they are here
            # because the guard's own installed copy is a file, and the tool
            # most obviously suited to replacing it was the one tool the hook
            # never saw (#222). Until this, `Write` to the pinned
            # fleet-secret-guard disabled the guard for every session on the
            # machine without the hook running at all.
            #
            # This is only half of that fix: the matcher in settings.json must
            # list Write|Edit too, or these branches are dead code. See the
            # Install block above — the two have to change together, and
            # tests/test_secret_guard.py::test_documented_matcher_covers_write
            # pins the documented form.
            target = str(tin.get("file_path") or tin.get("path")
                         or tin.get("notebook_path") or "")
            if target:
                forms = {os.path.expanduser(target), _norm(target)}
                for kind, name, rx in rules:
                    if kind == "path" and any(rx.search(f) for f in forms):
                        _block(name, "the target path")
        return 0
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as e:
        _log(f"WARN guard error ({e.__class__.__name__}: {e}) — failing open")
        return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
